1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,440
 The next property on our list is ductility, which is the capacity of a material to deform before

2
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:10,960
 fracturing. Very ductile materials will deform quite a long way before they finally snap.

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:15,200
 Very brittle materials won't deform very much at all before they snap.

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,040
 Ductility is generally considered a favorable property in engineering design,

5
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:23,120
 usually if something's going to fail, you want it to happen slowly enough that you can see it

6
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,880
 coming and make repairs if necessary. A brittle components can be quite scary and dangerous.

7
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:33,600
 Ductility is also quite important for many shaping processes and so if you'd like some way to

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:38,880
 work or shape or form your material after it's been made, then you probably want a ductile one.

9
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:42,640
 Often there's a trade-off between strength and ductility in materials design.

10
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:47,200
 There are some materials that have incredibly high strengths but aren't very ductile.

11
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,040
 Meaning that you can load them up with tons of stress and they'll be totally okay.

12
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:56,000
 But if you ever exceed their yield strength, they will snap, fail catastrophically.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,520
 On the other side of the coin, you might elect to use a material that has a lower yield

14
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:04,000
 strength but has some amount of ductility. Meaning that when it does start to fail,

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,400
 you can catch that, see it, make some sort of repair, replace the component, whatever.

16
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,080
 There's a brilliant video from Richard Hammond's engineering connections where they look at a

17
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:16,000
 steel versus a carbon fiber drive shaft that illustrates this perfectly.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,160
 The steel drive shaft doesn't have the strength that the carbon fiber one does but it is

19
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:25,040
 much more ductile. When it fails, it kind of deforms and twists around in a way that wouldn't hurt

20
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:30,400
 anybody. The carbon fiber one, when it fails, it brittle fails, it fractures and it does

21
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,560
 so in a way that could be quite dangerous if this component was actually in service.

22
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:39,360
 Ductility we measure using a quantity called percentage elongation which is really just a long

23
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:44,880
 way of saying strain at fracture. In other words, materials that are quite ductile strain

24
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:49,760
 quite a long way before they finally fracture. Materials which are brittle don't strain very far

25
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:54,400
 before they fracture. Generally speaking, and this is a broad generalization,

26
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:58,960
 metals and polymers are known for their ductility, much more so polymers than metals. On the other

27
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,520
 hand, ceramics and composites not so much.
